## Method
All steps run in one script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` environment (numpy, pandas, scikit-learn, matplotlib — all pinned in its lockfile; nothing is installed). Inputs are read-only.

1. Read the `counts_gene` matrix (columns `gene_id`, `gene_name`, then one column per sample) and the `design` table (`sample_id,condition,group,replicate`). Require that the sample columns and `sample_id` values match exactly; stop with an error otherwise.
2. Filter genes: keep genes with count >= 10 in at least 3 samples (the size of the smallest condition group).
3. Transform: `log2(count + 1)`. The matrix is salmon length-scaled counts (non-integer), so a VST/rlog from DESeq2 is not used; log2 stabilises variance well enough for a visual overview.
4. Select the 500 most variable genes by variance across samples of the log2 values.
5. PCA: centre each gene (no unit-variance scaling), compute with `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` (full SVD, `n_components` = 5, the maximum for 6 samples).
6. Plot PC1 vs PC2: one point per sample, coloured by `condition`, labelled with `sample_id`, axes annotated with percent variance explained, legend for condition. Colour-blind-safe two-colour palette; saved as PNG (200 dpi) and SVG.
7. Write the PC coordinates per sample (with condition) and the variance explained per PC to tables.

Not done: batch correction, outlier removal, differential expression, or interpretation of the result.

